1
00:00:01,040 --> 00:00:04,370
This video we're going to continue
our discussion of Linux File I/O, and

2
00:00:04,370 --> 00:00:06,991
cover the remaining portions
that we haven't covered in

3
00:00:06,991 --> 00:00:09,840
Linux System Programming chapter two.

4
00:00:09,840 --> 00:00:11,376
So some of the learning objectives,

5
00:00:11,376 --> 00:00:13,820
we're going to talk about how
to access file positionally.

6
00:00:13,820 --> 00:00:16,939
We're going to reintroduce the idea and
concept of Sparse Files,

7
00:00:16,939 --> 00:00:20,440
which we've actually mentioned
briefly in previous weeks.

8
00:00:20,440 --> 00:00:23,917
And then we're going to introduce
the idea of Multiplexed I/O.

9
00:00:23,917 --> 00:00:28,823
So reading positionally,
you might have had some experience with

10
00:00:28,823 --> 00:00:32,960
this already with the f versions
of open read and write.

11
00:00:32,960 --> 00:00:37,483
So if you want to rather than just read
from the beginning to the end of the file

12
00:00:37,483 --> 00:00:38,339
in sequence.

13
00:00:38,339 --> 00:00:42,895
If you need to change that up and need to
read from different parts of the file, or

14
00:00:42,895 --> 00:00:47,590
need to write to different certain areas
of the file, you can use lseek to do this.

15
00:00:47,590 --> 00:00:52,352
And there are a couple different options
in terms of how to locate within the file

16
00:00:52,352 --> 00:00:53,150
with lseek.

17
00:00:53,150 --> 00:00:57,299
And these are the same options that
are offered for the buffered I/O as well.

18
00:00:57,299 --> 00:01:01,811
So SEEK_SET means you're just
going to use the byte offset you

19
00:01:01,811 --> 00:01:03,942
mentioned inside the file.

20
00:01:03,942 --> 00:01:07,246
You're going to just use that relative
to the beginning of the file,

21
00:01:07,246 --> 00:01:10,374
that's going to be an absolute
position that you want to stick to.

22
00:01:10,374 --> 00:01:13,936
The SEEK_CUR says wherever I'm at,
I'm going to increment or

23
00:01:13,936 --> 00:01:17,094
decrement based on the offset
I specify into the file.

24
00:01:17,094 --> 00:01:21,581
And then SEEK_END means I'm going to
seek relative to the end of the file.

25
00:01:21,581 --> 00:01:26,361
So, if I say minus one, I'll be one
byte before the end of the file.

26
00:01:28,840 --> 00:01:32,840
The book also mentioned some alternatives
for positional reader, right?

27
00:01:32,840 --> 00:01:38,118
Based on the, and
an alternative to the lseek command or

28
00:01:38,118 --> 00:01:43,747
the lseek function I mentioned
earlier in period in pread.

29
00:01:43,747 --> 00:01:48,439
So, one benefit of these is if
you're using a the lseek command,

30
00:01:48,439 --> 00:01:51,598
you're going to be
modifying the position for

31
00:01:51,598 --> 00:01:55,372
any of the threads that
are running in your process.

32
00:01:55,372 --> 00:01:58,351
So you're basically setting
the position globally for

33
00:01:58,351 --> 00:02:00,940
your process on that file descriptor.

34
00:02:00,940 --> 00:02:04,795
But if you use the pread or pwrite
versions, you're not going to specify

35
00:02:04,795 --> 00:02:08,651
the origin, you're not going to
specify the location for all threads,

36
00:02:08,651 --> 00:02:12,898
you're going to be specifying the location
based on this one particular read or

37
00:02:12,898 --> 00:02:16,001
write command and
where it's going to go into the buffer.

38
00:02:16,001 --> 00:02:19,163
So, benefit would be if you're
using a multithreaded application,

39
00:02:19,163 --> 00:02:22,340
it's not going to manipulate
file position for other threads.

40
00:02:22,340 --> 00:02:26,524
And, in order to simplify this a bit,
the origin argument is removed and

41
00:02:26,524 --> 00:02:29,560
you're always going to use
the equivalent of six set.

42
00:02:29,560 --> 00:02:32,561
In other words, just an absolute
position into the file for locating.

43
00:02:34,440 --> 00:02:37,946
So talking a little bit
more about Sparse Files.

44
00:02:37,946 --> 00:02:44,301
If you use lseek and let's say you search
into a gigabyte offset based on SEEK_END.

45
00:02:44,301 --> 00:02:48,623
So in other words, I'm going to locate
from the end of the file, one gigabyte out

46
00:02:48,623 --> 00:02:52,370
on my current file descriptor and
then maybe I just write one value.

47
00:02:52,370 --> 00:02:57,340
Unsigned integer value let's say,
out to that location.

48
00:02:57,340 --> 00:03:03,040
This would extend the file, one gig past
the end of the current file location.

49
00:03:03,040 --> 00:03:06,587
So what if there's less than one
gigabyte of disk space available,

50
00:03:06,587 --> 00:03:09,040
would you expect this command to fail?

51
00:03:09,040 --> 00:03:12,090
And maybe you would,
just thinking about what it's doing.

52
00:03:12,090 --> 00:03:16,985
But in reality, the links file system
is smart enough to know that you didn't

53
00:03:16,985 --> 00:03:19,270
actually write a gigabyte of data.

54
00:03:19,270 --> 00:03:23,629
You just extended the file out that
long and wrote one byte past the end.

55
00:03:23,629 --> 00:03:29,070
So the Linux file system is actually smart
enough to not use disk space in this case.

56
00:03:29,070 --> 00:03:32,362
Instead it will create a hole to represent
the space in the file that hasn't

57
00:03:32,362 --> 00:03:33,540
been written.

58
00:03:33,540 --> 00:03:38,861
And if you read the file, it'll look
like it's a continuous block of values.

59
00:03:38,861 --> 00:03:42,365
But in reality when you're reading
the hole that hasn't been written,

60
00:03:42,365 --> 00:03:43,869
it'll just be returning 0s.

61
00:03:43,869 --> 00:03:47,361
And this is what's referred
to in Linux as a sparse file.

62
00:03:48,940 --> 00:03:53,080
So, if you want to see an example of
this again you can follow the link from

63
00:03:53,080 --> 00:03:54,500
the lecture notes.

64
00:03:54,500 --> 00:03:59,337
But here's a case where you can create
a sparse file using the sparse file dot c

65
00:03:59,337 --> 00:04:04,995
application, by doing an lseek relative to
SEEK_END based on the parameter specified,

66
00:04:04,995 --> 00:04:08,061
and then writing a single
byte at that location.

67
00:04:09,740 --> 00:04:14,930
So how can we see what's happened
here in terms of disk space usage?

68
00:04:14,930 --> 00:04:19,648
What you'll notice is that that sparse
file that we create, if you do ls- lh.

69
00:04:19,648 --> 00:04:21,435
So this is going to say list and

70
00:04:21,435 --> 00:04:25,740
give me a human readable
representation of the file size.

71
00:04:25,740 --> 00:04:30,456
What you'll notice is that the ls
command says that the file is

72
00:04:30,456 --> 00:04:32,548
954 megabytes in size.

73
00:04:32,548 --> 00:04:38,961
So roughly one gigabyte offset that we
specified here with sparse file parameter.

74
00:04:40,140 --> 00:04:43,419
But if I use du which is
the disk usage command, and

75
00:04:43,419 --> 00:04:47,557
I list the human readable size for
every file in the directory,

76
00:04:47,557 --> 00:04:52,270
I'll see that the sparse file is
actually only using 4k of disk space.

77
00:04:52,270 --> 00:04:55,461
So this is a way that you can tell that
the file is actually a sparse file.

78
00:04:58,040 --> 00:05:01,918
Okay, so if your,
let's say your application needs to handle

79
00:05:01,918 --> 00:05:05,010
input from more than one
file descriptor at once.

80
00:05:05,010 --> 00:05:07,453
And if you're using
something like a FIFO or

81
00:05:07,453 --> 00:05:09,706
a pipe as one of those file descriptors.

82
00:05:09,706 --> 00:05:13,628
And you would normally have a behavior
where you're blocking waiting for

83
00:05:13,628 --> 00:05:15,662
some input from the file descriptor.

84
00:05:15,662 --> 00:05:18,940
You have a couple of different
ways to handle this.

85
00:05:18,940 --> 00:05:22,500
So, two of the ways are the ones
that we've already talked about.

86
00:05:22,500 --> 00:05:25,497
One would be you just create a thread for
every descriptor and

87
00:05:25,497 --> 00:05:27,511
you just do the typical blocking reads.

88
00:05:27,511 --> 00:05:30,931
So, if you have some other work to
do you just spin up another thread.

89
00:05:30,931 --> 00:05:35,252
But you have a thread that's dedicated
to doing blocking reads on the FIFO or

90
00:05:35,252 --> 00:05:35,868
the pipe.

91
00:05:35,868 --> 00:05:38,842
And it's okay that it's blocking and
waiting for data.

92
00:05:38,842 --> 00:05:41,927
A second option would be that
you use nonblocking IO, or

93
00:05:41,927 --> 00:05:45,100
O_NONBLOCK that we talked
about in the previous video.

94
00:05:45,100 --> 00:05:47,004
And then you poll with your application.

95
00:05:47,004 --> 00:05:50,440
So you keep trying to read and
if there's data you do something with it.

96
00:05:50,440 --> 00:05:53,351
If there's not, you do something
else that the thread needs to do.

97
00:05:54,940 --> 00:06:00,370
But the book also mentions Multiplexed I/O
as a better solution than polling,

98
00:06:00,370 --> 00:06:05,311
because what it allows you to do is sleep
from one thread until a set of file

99
00:06:05,311 --> 00:06:07,280
descriptors is available.

100
00:06:07,280 --> 00:06:10,804
So in other words, rather than
needing to have a dedicated thread for

101
00:06:10,804 --> 00:06:14,998
every file descriptor, maybe you're in a
situation where you want to handle any of

102
00:06:14,998 --> 00:06:17,093
a bunch of different file descriptors.

103
00:06:17,093 --> 00:06:21,540
You want to handle IO and any of those
file descriptors whenever they occur.

104
00:06:21,540 --> 00:06:24,736
But you'd rather not have to spin up
a separate thread to monitor each one of

105
00:06:24,736 --> 00:06:25,940
them independently.

106
00:06:25,940 --> 00:06:30,613
And you'd rather not waste the cycles
of continuously polling and

107
00:06:30,613 --> 00:06:36,840
doing nothing other than polling every
one of the file descriptors forever.

108
00:06:36,840 --> 00:06:40,771
So there are three different options
to do this, select, pselect, and poll.

109
00:06:40,771 --> 00:06:44,570
The book mentions that poll is actually
the preferred mechanism of these,

110
00:06:44,570 --> 00:06:46,692
based on the flexibility that it offers.

111
00:06:46,692 --> 00:06:49,169
But all of them essentially
do the same thing,

112
00:06:49,169 --> 00:06:52,536
which is take a list of file
descriptors that you want to watch and

113
00:06:52,536 --> 00:06:56,477
sleep until something happens on one
of those file descriptors, at which

114
00:06:56,477 --> 00:07:00,951
point you'll get a notification from your
application that you need to handle it.